SLIDE 1 : ASR Context & The Storage Challenge
How Azure Site Recovery (ASR) Works: ASR provides continuous disaster recovery by capturing VM disk writes in production, sending them to a temporary cache, and then applying them to passive "replica disks" in the DR region.

The Default Behavior: By default, ASR strictly mirrors the production infrastructure. If a production VM uses a high-performance Premium SSD, ASR automatically provisions an identical Premium SSD in the DR region to hold the replicated data.

The Financial Waste: Managed disks are billed on provisioned capacity, not usage. This means we are paying top-tier prices for high IOPS and throughput on passive, dormant disks that only receive background replication data.

SLIDE 2 : The Optimized Architecture (Smart Tiering)
The Solution: We will decouple the target storage. Since our monitoring shows the average data churn is well below 8 MB/s (1), Azure fully supports replicating this data directly onto highly cost-effective Standard HDDs.

Zero Performance Loss on DR: We do not sacrifice production performance during an actual disaster.

We will integrate a PowerShell Runbook via Azure Automation into our ASR Recovery Plan. During a failover event, this script automatically updates the disk SKU from Standard HDD back to Premium SSD while the VM boots up.

